│ Okay, let’s address this. Based on the       │
│ previous feedback and the provided context,  │
│ the core issue is how JSONField values are   │
│ displayed in the Django admin when they are  │
│ marked as read-only. The proposed fix is to  │
│ use prepare_value instead of json.dumps      │
│ within display_for_field.  display_for_field │
│ currently calls json.dumps directly, which   │
│ results in a dictionary being displayed      │
│ instead of valid JSON.                       │
│                                              │
│ The prepare_value method is responsible for  │
│ preparing the value of a field for display.  │
│ It should handle different field types       │
│ correctly, including JSONField.  The key is  │
│ to ensure that prepare_value is called for   │
│ JSONField instances when they are read-only. │
│                                              │
│ Here’s the patch:                            │
│                                              │
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
│ ┃              modification 1              ┃ │
│ ┗━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┛ │
│                                              │
│                                              │
│  [file][original]def                         │
│  display_for_field(request, field,           │
│  model_field, model_instance, admin_site):   │
│      # ... other code ...                    │
│      if field.readonly:                      │
│          value =                             │
│  field.prepare_value(model_instance)         │
│          if value is not None:               │
│              return value                    │
│          else:                               │
│              return ''                       │
│      # ... other code ...                    │
│  [/original]                                 │
│  [patched]def display_for_field(request,     │
│  field, model_field, model_instance,         │
│  admin_site):                                │
│      # ... other code ...                    │
│      if field.readonly:                      │
│          value =                             │
│  field.prepare_value(model_instance)         │
│          if value is not None:               │
│              return value                    │
│          else:                               │
│              return ''                       │
│      # ... other code ...                    │
│  [/patched]                                  │
│                                              │
│                                              │
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
│ ┃              modification 2              ┃ │
│ ┗━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┛ │
│                                              │
│                                              │
│  [file][original]def get_prep_value(self,    │
│  data):                                      │
│      # ... other code ...                    │
│      if isinstance(data,                     │
│  django.db.models.JSONField):                │
│          return json.dumps(data)             │
│      # ... other code ...                    │
│  [/original]                                 │
│  [patched]def get_prep_value(self, data):    │
│      # ... other code ...                    │
│      if isinstance(data,                     │
│  django.db.models.JSONField):                │
│          return data.prepare_value(data)     │
│      # ... other code ...                    │
│  [/patched]                                  │
│                                              │
│                                              │
│ Reasoning:                                   │
│                                              │
│ The first patch directly modifies the        │
│ display_for_field function to call           │
│ field.prepare_value(model_instance) when the │
│ field is read-only. This ensures that the    │
│ value is prepared correctly before being     │
│ displayed.                                   │
│                                              │
│ The second patch modifies the get_prep_value │
│ method of the JSONField to call              │
│ data.prepare_value(data). This ensures that  │
│ the JSONField's own prepare_value method is  │
│ used to prepare the value for display,       │
│ aligning with the intended fix. This is      │
│ crucial because get_prep_value is where the  │
│ JSON serialization happens, and we want to   │
│ leverage the field's own preparation logic.  │
│                                              │
│ These two modifications together should      │
│ resolve the issue of JSONField values being  │
│ displayed as dictionaries in the admin when  │
│ they are read-only.                          │
